Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama, naturalistic faces, fabric, weather, and spectral effects.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Ebenezer Scrooge, a thin elderly man with a pointed nose, red eyes, blue lips, white-rimed brows, and a black greatcoat, counts coins beside a miserly fire.

(SOUND: wind wheezes through window cracks, quills scratch, distant city clocks strike three)

Bob Cratchit, a gentle young clerk in a threadbare coat and long white comforter, copies letters in the cold adjoining tank.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas but a time for paying bills without money? A time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

FRED
I have always thought of it as a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time.

FRED
It is the only time when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely.

Bob involuntarily claps once, then pokes his tiny fire until its last spark dies.

(SOUND: poker scrapes iron, one frail coal hisses out)

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation.

FRED
Come and dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I ask nothing of you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

Fred leaves warmly, exchanging a nod with Bob at the door.

MR. HARKER, a portly charitable gentleman in a dark wool coat, enters with a collection book.

MR. HARKER
Many thousands are in want of common necessaries. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing.

MR. HARKER
Many can't go to the workhouses. Many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

MR. HARKER
But you might know it.

SCROOGE
It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen.

Mr. Harker closes his book and withdraws.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
Be here all the earlier next morning.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS EVE

Scrooge, now in a worn black dressing-gown, sits near a low fire with a bowl of gruel. Dutch tiles ring the hearth; a disused bell hangs overhead.

(SOUND: spoon taps bowl, shutters rattle, the house settles)

The bell trembles, then swings harder. Every bell in the building answers.

(SOUND: bells clang through the house, followed by a heavy chain dragging up stairs)

SCROOGE
Humbug still! I won't believe it.

The locked door bursts open without moving. Jacob Marley enters: transparent, grey-faced, in his old waistcoat and pigtail, bound in a chain of cash-boxes, keys, ledgers, and padlocks.

(SOUND: steel chain clanks across bare boards, fire flares sharply)

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you, then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese.

SCROOGE
There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley tears the bandage from his jaw. His lower jaw falls against his breast.

(SOUND: Marley gives a piercing cry; the chain crashes against the hearthstone)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

MARLEY
Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were all my business.

SCROOGE
You were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

Marley raises his chain. Scrooge drops to his knees.

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Expect the first when the bell tolls One.

MARLEY
The second on the next night at the same hour.

MARLEY
The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate.

The window rises. Beyond it, chained phantoms drift through the fog, reaching helplessly toward unseen people below.

(SOUND: spectral wailing rides the winter wind, chains fading into mist)

MARLEY
Look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

Marley floats out into the night. Scrooge stares after him, then collapses onto his bed without undressing.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The clock face glows midnight-blue. Scrooge wakes in his dressing-gown as a deep bell tolls one.

(SOUND: a church bell tolls one; bed curtains slide on their rings)

A childlike yet ancient figure stands beside him: white hair, smooth young face, white tunic, holly branch, summer flowers, and a bright flame issuing from its crown.

(SOUND: a soft crystalline hum surrounds the Spirit)

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brought you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation. Rise, and walk with me.

The Spirit grips Scrooge's arm and leads him through the bedroom wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - WINTER DAY

Snow covers the fields around a country road. Young boys race ponies toward a distant market town, shouting Christmas greetings.

(SOUND: pony hooves crunch snow, boys call across the fields)

Scrooge, still elderly and dressed in his black robe, recognizes each gate and tree.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

INT. BOARDING-SCHOOL ROOM - WINTER DAY

A bare schoolroom holds rows of plain desks. Young Ebenezer, a small pale boy with dark hair and an oversized wool jacket, reads alone beside a feeble fire.

(SOUND: wind moans through cracked panes, mice scratch behind panelling)

Scrooge watches his younger self. Tears wet his lined face.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

FAN, a small bright-eyed girl in a blue wool cloak, rushes in and throws her arms around Young Ebenezer.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother!

YOUNG EBENEZER
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be.

FAN
We're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

Young Ebenezer takes her hand. Their school trunk is carried away as they hurry toward a waiting carriage.

SCROOGE
She had a large heart.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had one child.

SCROOGE
My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE, YEARS EARLIER

A bright warehouse has been cleared for dancing. Young Ebenezer, now a slim young man with dark hair and a modest brown waistcoat, raises shutters beside cheerful Dick Wilkins.

(SOUND: shutters slam up, fiddle tunes, dancers laugh)

Fezziwig, a round elderly man in a Welsh wig and broad waistcoat, claps his hands.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer!

The workers whirl through a country dance. Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig dance in the center, joyful and tireless.

(SOUND: fiddle plays “Sir Roger de Coverley,” boots stamp in rhythm)

Scrooge laughs with the remembered young Ebenezer, then falls still.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small! He has the power to render us happy or unhappy.

SCROOGE
The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge turns away, ashamed.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT, YEARS EARLIER

Belle, a young woman with braided dark hair and a plain mourning dress, sits opposite Young Ebenezer. A small fire burns between them.

(SOUND: clock ticks steadily, rain taps the window)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG EBENEZER
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG EBENEZER
There is nothing condemned with such severity as the pursuit of wealth.

BELLE
You fear the world too much.

BELLE
Your nobler aspirations have fallen off one by one, until Gain engrosses you.

YOUNG EBENEZER
I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
You are changed. When our contract was made, you were another man.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle rises and leaves. The room darkens around Young Ebenezer.

(SOUND: the door closes softly; the fire gives a final crack)

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more.

INT. BELLE'S FAMILY PARLOUR - CHRISTMAS EVE, YEARS LATER

Belle, now a warm-faced matron, sits amid laughing children around a crowded fire. Her husband arrives carrying parcels.

(SOUND: children shriek with delight, paper parcels rustle, a door knocks)

BELLE'S HUSBAND
I passed an old friend of yours this afternoon.

BELLE
Who was it?

BELLE'S HUSBAND
Mr. Scrooge. His partner lies upon the point of death.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
There he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

Scrooge recoils. The Ghost's crown-light burns brighter.

SCROOGE
Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

Scrooge seizes the Spirit's extinguisher-cap and forces it over the flame.

(SOUND: a rush of light hisses beneath the cap; Scrooge's breath turns ragged)

The Spirit vanishes beneath it. Scrooge reels backward into darkness.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A ruddy glow spills beneath Scrooge's door. He opens it into his own transformed room, now a green bower piled with food and blazing with fire.

(SOUND: hearth roars, chestnuts pop, distant bells toll one)

The Ghost of Christmas Present sits amid turkeys, puddings, fruit, and steaming punch. He is a towering, broad-chested giant with long brown curls, a holly crown, a green fur-trimmed robe, and a glowing torch.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe.

Scrooge grips the green robe. The feast, fire, and room vanish.

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS MORNING

London shines beneath fresh snow. Shoppers crowd around bright grocers' windows, carrying baskets toward bakers and homes.

(SOUND: snow shovels scrape stone, cart wheels slosh, church bells ring)

The Ghost sprinkles light from his torch over two men who jostle, and their angry faces soften.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE
Why to a poor one most?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Because it needs it most.

The Spirit guides Scrooge toward a narrow row of modest houses.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - CHRISTMAS DAY

A small four-room home glows with a hardworking fire. Mrs. Cratchit, a practical woman in a twice-turned dress with bright ribbons, sets plates while Martha and Peter prepare dinner.

(SOUND: potatoes bubble beneath a lid, kettle whistles, children chatter)

Two younger Cratchits race in.

YOUNG CRATCHIT
We smelt the goose outside the baker's! We knew it was ours!

The door opens. Bob enters in his threadbare coat and white comforter, carrying Tiny Tim on his shoulder.

Tiny Tim is a frail little boy with soft brown curls, a patched green jacket, an iron leg brace, and a small wooden crutch.

(SOUND: Tiny Tim's crutch taps the floor; the family cheers)

BOB
And how did little Tim behave?

TINY TIM
As good as gold, and better.

BOB
He hoped the people saw him in church, because he was a cripple.

BOB
It might be pleasant to them to remember who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family gathers around a small goose. Mrs. Cratchit plunges in the carving knife; stuffing spills out.

(SOUND: knives and forks tap plates; a joyous murmur rises around the table)

Later, Mrs. Cratchit brings in a blazing pudding crowned with holly.

(SOUND: brandy flames crackle; children gasp and clap)

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Scrooge watches Tiny Tim grip Bob's hand.

SCROOGE
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge lowers his eyes, struck by his own words.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

BOB
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

The children drink reluctantly. Tiny Tim drinks last.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

Fred, ruddy-faced and handsome in an evening waistcoat, laughs beside his wife LUCY, a lively young woman in a rose-colored silk gown. Friends gather around a fire and dessert table.

(SOUND: laughter fills the room, glasses clink, a harp plays softly nearby)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

LUCY
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
His wealth is of no use to him. He does no good with it.

FRED
Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always.

The guests begin a guessing game. Fred's laughter becomes contagious.

(SOUND: feet stamp during blind-man's buff; laughter crescendos)

LUCY
I have found it out! I know what it is, Fred!

FRED
What is it?

LUCY
It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

The company bursts into delighted laughter.

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment. It would be ungrateful not to drink his health.

FRED
A merry Christmas and a happy new year to the old man, whatever he is!

Scrooge smiles through tears and lifts an invisible glass.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Fred.

EXT. LONDON STREET - NIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present has grown old and grey. Snow drifts through a deserted street beneath midnight bells.

(SOUND: wind rises through alleyways; bells strike three quarters past eleven)

Two ragged children crawl from beneath the Spirit's green robe: Ignorance, a yellow-faced boy, and Want, a gaunt girl in torn grey clothing.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no workhouses?

The final stroke of twelve sounds. The Ghost vanishes.

(SOUND: midnight's last bell vibration dies into a low, hollow wind)

A tall, hooded Phantom in a black shroud glides from the fog, one pale hand extended.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen.

SCROOGE
Lead on. The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me.

EXT. EXCHANGE COURTYARD - DAY

Merchants in black coats cluster in the cold courtyard, fingering watch seals and coins. The hooded Ghost stands behind Scrooge.

(SOUND: coins chink, boots cross wet cobblestones, carriage wheels rattle)

MR. BARKER, a heavy merchant with a broad chin, speaks to MR. SLOANE, a red-faced businessman.

MR. BARKER
I only know he's dead.

MR. SLOANE
When did he die?

MR. BARKER
Last night, I believe.

MR. SLOANE
What has he done with his money?

MR. BARKER
Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me.

The merchants laugh.

(SOUND: brief harsh laughter, then a clock bell strikes)

MR. BARKER
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. I don't know of anybody to go to it.

Scrooge looks toward the Phantom, but it only points onward.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG SHOP - NIGHT

A filthy shop is piled with keys, chains, rags, bottles, and rusted scales. Old Joe, a grey-haired fence with a pipe, sits by a charcoal stove.

(SOUND: rain drips through the roof, stove coals crackle, rusty hinges screech)

Mrs. Dilber, a laundress, and CHARWOMAN NELL, a sharp-faced woman with a heavy bundle, throw stolen goods onto the floor.

NELL
What's the odds, Mrs. Dilber? Every person has a right to take care of themselves.

MRS. DILBER
He always did.

NELL
If he had been natural in his lifetime, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death.

Old Joe unrolls bed-curtains and a fine shirt.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

NELL
Yes, I took them down, rings and all. Why not?

OLD JOE
You were born to make your fortune.

NELL
He isn't likely to take cold without them, I dare say.

The three laugh as Old Joe counts coins into their hands.

(SOUND: coins clatter on a wooden table; their laughter turns shrill)

NELL
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

SCROOGE
Spirit! The case of this unhappy man might be my own.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare bed stands in a dark, stripped room. A body lies under a ragged sheet, unwatched and unwept.

(SOUND: a cat claws at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearthstone)

The Ghost points to the covered head. Scrooge cannot lift the sheet.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

The Cratchit home is quieter than before. Mrs. Cratchit sews by the hearth. Peter reads while the younger children sit motionless.

(SOUND: clock ticks, sewing needle pierces cloth, fire murmurs low)

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

PETER
They're better now again, mother.

The door opens. Bob enters, older in grief but still wearing his patched coat and long white comforter.

(SOUND: the crutch is absent; Bob's slow boots stop at the threshold)

BOB
I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is.

BOB
I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday.

Bob climbs to Tiny Tim's empty chair, laid beside a small bed. He kisses the child's unseen face.

(SOUND: Bob's muffled sob; distant church bells drift through the window)

BOB
My little, little child!

Downstairs, the family gathers around him.

BOB
When we recollect how patient and mild he was, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves.

PETER
Never, father.

BOB
I am very happy.

Scrooge stands shaken beside the Phantom.

SCROOGE
Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A neglected churchyard lies walled between dark houses. Weeds cover crooked stones. The hooded Ghost points toward one grave.

(SOUND: winter wind shakes bare branches; a distant funeral bell tolls)

Scrooge approaches, trembling. Snow reveals the inscription: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

(SOUND: Scrooge's breath catches; the bell tolls again)

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The Ghost points from the grave to Scrooge and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

SCROOGE
Men's courses foreshadow certain ends, if persevered in.

SCROOGE
But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Ghost remains still.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future.

SCROOGE
Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

Scrooge clutches the Phantom's hand. The black shroud collapses into darkness.

(SOUND: a rushing gale swallows the bell; the stone cracks like ice)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge wakes clutching his own bedpost. His black dressing-gown is twisted around him; tears shine on his pale cheeks.

(SOUND: jubilant church bells ring outside; bright morning birds call)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future!

SCROOGE
The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me!

He laughs, opens the window, and sunlight fills the room.

(SOUND: window sash flies up; bells clash gloriously across London)

TOM, a quick-eyed boy in Sunday clothes, looks up from the street.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TOM
To-day! Why, Christmas Day.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one?

TOM
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
Go and buy the big prize Turkey. Tell them to bring it here.

SCROOGE
Come back with the man in less than five minutes, and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Tom races away. Scrooge pats the old door knocker affectionately.

SCROOGE
I shall love it as long as I live. What an honest expression it has in its face!

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

Scrooge, now dressed in his best black coat, silk hat, and clean white cravat, walks through clear Christmas sunlight with a delighted smile.

(SOUND: carolers sing nearby, horses clip-clop, bells continue joyfully)

He sees Mr. Harker, still carrying his collection book, and hurries to him.

SCROOGE
My dear sir! A merry Christmas to you!

MR. HARKER
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Allow me to ask your pardon.

Scrooge whispers an amount in Mr. Harker's ear.

(SOUND: Mr. Harker inhales sharply; Scrooge's cane taps the pavement)

MR. HARKER
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

MR. HARKER
I don't know what to say.

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

The dining table is laid for Christmas supper. Fred and Lucy inspect the dishes when Scrooge opens the door and peeks around it.

(SOUND: plates settle on the table, a fire crackles, guests laugh in another room)

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred rushes forward and clasps Scrooge's hand.

(SOUND: chairs scrape, laughter and greetings rise around them)

FRED
Let you in? With all my heart!

Lucy embraces Scrooge. He takes his place among them as the party gathers.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - MORNING

The counting-house is brighter than before. Scrooge sits at his desk in his black coat, door wide open. A new coal-scuttle rests near Bob's stove.

(SOUND: clock strikes nine, street traffic hums, coal drops into a lively fire)

Bob enters eighteen minutes late, breathless, in his threadbare coat and long white comforter.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob approaches cautiously, eyeing the ruler.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

Scrooge leaps up, grips Bob by the waistcoat, and beams.

(SOUND: Bob gasps; Scrooge laughs warmly)

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB
Sir?

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year!

SCROOGE
I'll assist your struggling family. Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i!

Bob stares, then laughs through tears. Scrooge feeds coal into both fires.

(SOUND: both fires roar alive; the counting-house clock chimes brightly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well.

FADE OUT.

THE END
